{# THE ONLY TEMPLATE IN THIS THEME WITH A `<tr>`, and the place the cell macro lives.

   A LISTING WITH COLUMNS, AND IT OWNS ITS ROWS. `columns` is the ordered column
   set, `rows` is the data, and a `<td>` is emitted PER COLUMN, addressed by that
   column's `key`. That keying is the whole change: a row cannot carry a cell for
   a column that does not exist, and a column with no cell renders an empty `<td>`
   instead of shifting every value one place to the left. Five row components used
   to own these cells between them, which is how three headers came to sit over
   rows that emitted nine `<td>` each on a delivered screen — invalid markup that
   rendered without complaint, because the container could not see its own cells.

   `rows` IS A PROP AND NOT A SLOT. A slot is how the previous architecture
   injected an already-rendered row, and the `head`/`body` slots are gone for that
   reason alone. What makes the prop legal is that a cell is ADDRESSED BY ITS
   COLUMN: eight of the nine kinds below are a handful of strings, and the ninth
   — `rendered` — carries markup a field formatter already produced, but it is
   still keyed against a column and still emits exactly one `<td>`. That is the
   whole difference from a slot, which would let a caller inject a `<td>` no
   column asked for. The one slot left is `empty`, the collapsed variant's
   confirmation strip — a sentence a screen writes, not a value a listing
   carries.

   HOW A DRUPAL VIEW BINDS THIS, since that is now a real caller rather than a
   hypothetical — and the caller is EVERY table view in this theme, not one
   screen. `views-view-table.html.twig` is a GLOBAL style template with no
   per-view suffix: it maps Views' own `header` onto `columns` and each row's
   rendered field output onto a `rendered` cell keyed by the field id. So a
   table view gets this component by existing. The ticket list's nine real Views
   fields and the delivered team members screen (`views.view.bb_team_members`)
   both arrive here through that one file.

   THERE IS NO PER-VIEW PHP, AND THAT IS THE POINT. No preprocess hook, no
   style plugin, nothing to alter per view — the component fits any table view
   with nothing to maintain alongside it, which is the property that was asked
   for rather than one that happened. It is recorded in the component and not
   only in the binding because it is exactly what a later “just add a
   preprocess for this one view” would quietly destroy: the markup would still
   render, every gate would still pass, and the guarantee would be gone.

   WHAT THE TEMPLATE CANNOT DERIVE, IT ASKS THE ROW ENTITY — AND IT ASKS
   WITHOUT AN INTERFACE. This paragraph named `BbTableRowInterface` until
   2026-09-04, and that class is DELETED: the human's instruction was that it
   "must be gone", and `TableRowFaceTest` fails if it comes back. What the
   binding does now is ask the entity for its phase and let `|default('')`
   absorb a missing method — an entity that cannot answer declines, and
   declining needs no contract to be legal. The team screen's `og_membership`
   rows render with no accent while a ticket's rows carry one, out of the same
   template and with no branch that knows which is which, and an absent `accent`
   falls through to the transparent rail.

   THE ROW TARGET WENT WITH THE INTERFACE, and this component still HAS the
   prop. `views-view-table.html.twig` used to ask `getBbRowUrl` and pass the
   answer; it does not any more, measured before deleting it — no JavaScript and
   no CSS in this theme read `data-row-url`, and its only readers were the tests
   asserting it. So `url` is a prop no caller in the product fills, and the
   `{% if row.url %}` below is dead on every live surface while remaining true
   of the component: hand a row a `url` and it still emits the attribute, which
   is what `table.delivery.story.yml` and `table.unassigned.story.yml` exercise.
   It is kept rather than deleted because the component's contract is not the
   binding's, and it is described as unfilled rather than as live because a
   reader who believed the old sentence would go looking for the view that
   supplies it.

   SO VIEWS OWNS the column set, the per-field access check and the sort
   affordance; each column renders itself through its own formatter; and the
   faces still live in one place, because a formatter reaches `cell()` by
   component id — an `{% import 'bb_theme:table' %}` resolves from a render
   array exactly as it resolves from `list`. That is what keeps a badge in a
   table cell and a badge produced by a field formatter one code path and not
   two that agree.

   WHERE THE MACRO LIVES, AND WHY IT LIVES HERE. `cell()` is declared by this
   template and imported by `list` through `{% import 'bb_theme:table' %}`, so
   exactly one place in the theme knows what a badge cell or a person cell looks
   like. It is not a bare `components/_cell.twig`, and that is a resolution fact
   rather than a preference: Drupal's `ComponentLoader` resolves ONLY component
   ids, and its `@bb_theme` namespace points at `templates/` and at the theme
   root — never at `components/` — while the Storybook loader points the same
   namespace AT `components/`. So `@bb_theme/_cell.twig` names two different
   files in the two environments and a component id names the same file in both.
   The component id is the only portable address a shared macro can have, so the
   macro lives inside a component, and it lives inside this one because a table
   cell is the shape it was written for. Importing a template runs none of its
   body, so `list` pays nothing for the import but the macro.

   THE SORTABLE HEADER IS WHAT MAKES THIS A TABLE rather than a list with lines,
   and it survives this rewrite unchanged — it only moved from `headers` to
   `columns`.

   EVERY OPTIONAL CELL FIELD IS GUARDED IN `cell()`, and the guard is load-bearing
   rather than tidy. A cell that omits `icon`, `suffix`, `mail`, `clear_label`,
   `create_label` or `picker_id` used to forward NULL to a prop the composed
   component types as a string, and core's validator rejects exactly that:
   "[bb_theme:badge/icon] NULL value found, but a string or an object is
   required." It never surfaced on the running site for one reason only — the
   ui_patterns node visitor unsets NULL props before the validator sees them — so
   this component was rendering its own optional props on a contrib module's side
   effect. A `|default('')` on each of the six ends that dependency and changes no
   output, because every consumer already reads an empty string as absent: badge
   tests `icon` and `suffix` for truth, person tests `mail`, `option_list` falls
   back to `— none —` and `+ New item`, and `option_picker` falls back to its own
   base id.

   WHERE EACH CELL KIND IS EXERCISED, because a keyed cell set is only as good as
   its coverage, and no single story shows all eight. `default` is this
   component's REFERENCE render — the nine-column ticket list — and it carries
   five kinds: `mono` for the identifier, `text` in all three emphases, `badge`
   plain and with an `icon`, a `dot` and a `suffix`, `person`, and `link`. It is
   also the only story that shows all three sort states side by side — `asc` (this
   IS the sort column), `none` (sortable, not current) and `sort` ABSENT (not
   sortable) — with `num: true` on the column that is read down rather than
   across, and its claim cell carries the whole lease badge in both of its
   answers: `tone: lease` with `mono: true`, and `mine: true` where the lease is
   the reader's own rather than somebody else's. `picker` and `masked` are
   exercised by the `list` stories, which are the surfaces that carry them.
   `rendered` is covered by a TEST and not by a story, which is a deliberate
   choice and is recorded rather than left to be discovered: a story would show
   only that markup appears, while the thing that can actually rot is that a
   `MarkupInterface` prints once where a plain string does not. Telling those
   two apart needs two renders compared against each other, and needs core's
   validator called directly, because the Storybook build validates no props at
   all.
   `bare` is the cover for the guards above: one row whose badge, person and
   picker cells omit all six optional fields, so an edit that drops a guard has
   something to fail against. `wired` is the cover for the form-wired options:
   a `picker` cell with a submitted `name` and an `action` cell with `href` and
   `hx`. #}
{% import _self as cells %}

{% macro cell(c) %}
  {#- ONE CELL, NINE KINDS, and the only place any of them is decided.

      `text` IS THE `else` BRANCH DELIBERATELY. An unknown `kind`, or a cell with
      no `kind` at all, renders its value as plain text — so a mapping mistake
      shows up as unstyled content that a reviewer sees, rather than as an empty
      cell that looks like missing data. Failing visibly beats failing silently in
      a layer nothing type-checks.

      IT COMPOSES `badge`, `person`, `option_picker`/`option_list` and `button`,
      AND NOTHING ELSE. Every other kind is a span this macro owns outright. A
      value shape the nine cannot express is the signal to add a TENTH kind here,
      deliberately — never to reintroduce a slot, which would buy back exactly the
      freedom the container exists to remove. That rule has been exercised twice
      now. `action` is the eighth: seven kinds rendered a value the record HOLDS
      and none of them was a control, so a row action had nowhere to go.
      `rendered` is the ninth: a Views field decides its own face through its
      formatter and hands this template finished markup rather than data. Neither
      is a slot, and the difference is not cosmetic — a slot injects a whole
      `<td>` and escapes the keying, while both of these are addressed by a
      column and emit exactly the one `<td>` that column asked for. `status` is
      the tenth: a workflow state shown as a glyph rather than a word, which a
      badge (label required, text first) cannot be. #}
  {%- set emphasis_map = {
    'faint': 'text-text-faint',
    'strong': 'font-semibold text-text'
  } -%}
  {%- if c.kind == 'mono' -%}
    {#- A machine name: an id, a branch, a key. The FACE is the information — it
        tells a reader to match the string character by character instead of
        reading it — which is why the face is a kind of its own and not an
        emphasis. #}
    <span class="font-mono text-data text-text-light">{{ c.value }}</span>
  {%- elseif c.kind == 'link' -%}
    {#- The row's VISIBLE affordance. A `<tr>` cannot be wrapped in an anchor and
        inventing one inside a cell would add a `<td>` no column asked for, so a
        row that is reachable says so in a column of its own. Underline on hover
        AND on focus-visible: a keyboard reader gets the same signal as a mouse.
        `full_load` opts the anchor out of the island's htmx boost, so a link to
        a FORM PAGE is a full document load and the form's `#action` placeholder
        is replaced instead of posting to a 404 (see the prop's description). #}
    {%- if c.external -%}
      {#- An address outside bakers.build: styled like a prose link, opened apart. #}
      <a class="text-body text-link underline underline-offset-2 hover:decoration-2 focus-visible:outline-2 focus-visible:outline-offset-2 focus-visible:outline-focus-ring break-all" href="{{ c.url|default('#') }}" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">{{ c.value }}</a>
    {%- else -%}
    <a class="{{ c.mono ? 'font-mono text-data' : 'text-body' }} text-text no-underline hover:underline focus-visible:outline-2 focus-visible:outline-offset-2 focus-visible:outline-focus-ring"{% if c.full_load %} data-hx-boost="false"{% endif %} href="{{ c.url|default('#') }}">{{ c.value }}</a>
    {%- endif -%}
  {%- elseif c.kind == 'badge' -%}
    {#- With `url` a badge is a linked chip; hover dims it, because underline
        does not paint through an inline-flex child. #}
    {%- if c.url is defined and c.url is not empty -%}
    <a class="inline-flex rounded-sm no-underline transition-opacity duration-fast hover:opacity-80 focus-visible:outline-2 focus-visible:outline-offset-2 focus-visible:outline-focus-ring"{% if c.full_load %} data-hx-boost="false"{% endif %} href="{{ c.url }}">
    {%- endif -%}
    {{ include('bb_theme:badge', {
      label: c.label,
      tone: c.tone|default('neutral'),
      dot: c.dot|default(false),
      icon: c.icon|default(''),
      suffix: c.suffix|default(''),
      suffix_datetime: c.suffix_datetime|default(''),
      suffix_title: c.suffix_title|default(''),
      mono: c.mono|default(false),
      mine: c.mine|default(false),
    }, with_context = false) }}
    {%- if c.url is defined and c.url is not empty %}</a>{% endif -%}
  {%- elseif c.kind == 'person' -%}
    {#- THE VARIANT IS DERIVED FROM `mail`, and that is the cell's field list
        talking rather than cleverness. `person` itself says `full` is "an identity
        that is the subject of its row" and `compact` is "an identity that is a
        control or a cell"; a cell that was handed a mail address is the first kind
        and one that was not is the second. Hard-coding `compact` would silently
        drop the mail a members list passes, and adding a `variant` field to the
        cell would let a mapping ask for a two-line identity in a table row. #}
    {#- `picture`, `image_fit` and `size` pass through: a listing row's leading
        avatar is a person cell with no name, an image and `size: md`. #}
    {#- `name_hidden` keeps the circle alone, a row's right-hand assignee: the
        name moves into the tooltip and, through `role="img"`, into the
        accessible name, so the person is still named rather than only coloured. #}
    {%- set subject = c.mail is not empty -%}
    {%- set name_hidden = c.name_hidden|default(false) and not subject -%}
    {{ include('bb_theme:person', {
      attributes: name_hidden ? create_attribute({ 'role': 'img', 'aria-label': c.display_name|default(c.initials), 'title': c.display_name|default(c.initials) }) : null,
      variant: subject ? 'full' : 'compact',
      size: c.size|default(subject ? 'md' : 'sm'),
      display_name: name_hidden ? '' : c.display_name|default(''),
      initials: c.initials,
      color: c.color|default('running'),
      mail: c.mail|default(''),
      picture: c.picture|default(''),
      image_fit: c.image_fit|default('cover'),
    }, with_context = false) }}
  {%- elseif c.kind == 'status' -%}
    {#- Working vs not: a filled disc while the row is in coding, an empty
        dashed ring otherwise. The rail carries occupancy and review; this
        glyph does not. The exact state label is the tooltip and, via
        `role="img"`, the accessible name. #}
    {%- set working = c.working|default(false) -%}
    <span role="img" aria-label="{{ c.label }}" title="{{ c.label }}" class="inline-flex shrink-0 items-center {{ working ? 'text-text' : 'text-text-faint' }}">
      {{- include('bb_theme:icon', { name: working ? 'status-running' : 'status-prepare', size: 'lg' }, with_context = false) -}}
    </span>
  {%- elseif c.kind == 'picker' -%}
    {#- THE ONE INTERACTIVE CELL, and it needs no new rule: the values it offers
        are reversible, so `action-pattern.md` › *Inline edit* applies unchanged —
        the pick IS the write, no save button and no confirmation. The inline
        variant shows no visible field name, so the accessible name carries both
        the field and its current value; the panel markup comes from `option_list`
        so it exists in one place only.

        `multiple` DEFAULTS TO FALSE and is not derived from how many values
        happen to be picked. `aria-multiselectable` is a statement about the FIELD,
        and a multi-value field holding one value would otherwise be announced as
        a single choice.

        A PICKER CELL CARRIES WHAT THE PICKER NEEDS, and the five fields below are
        why. `clearable`/`clear_label` and `creatable`/`create_label` are declared
        by `option_list`, because a clear row and a create row ARE rows of the
        listbox (`option_list` says so at length); `icon` is declared by
        `option_picker`, because the icon sits on the trigger. So each field goes
        to whichever of the two owns it — the cell is the union of both contracts,
        not a guess at one. Without `clearable` a field cannot be EMPTIED from a
        rail at all: the clear row is the only unset affordance the design has,
        and its absence is a lost capability rather than a lost decoration.

        `name` is the submitted field name, so the cell can live in a real form:
        `option_list` renders real inputs under it and a clearable clear row.
        Without it the name is derived from the label, for inert scenes. The
        accessible name keeps reading the label. #}
    {%- set field = c.name|default(c.label|lower) -%}
    {%- set spoken = c.label|lower -%}
    {%- set picked = c.value_labels|default([]) -%}
    {%- set panel %}
      {{- include('bb_theme:option_list', {
        multiple: c.multiple|default(false),
        name: field,
        input_name: c.name|default(''),
        clear_control: c.name is not empty,
        options: c.options|default([]),
        clearable: c.clearable|default(false),
        clear_label: c.clear_label|default(''),
        creatable: c.creatable|default(false),
        create_label: c.create_label|default(''),
      }, with_context = false) -}}
    {% endset -%}
    {{ include('bb_theme:option_picker', {
      variant: 'inline',
      label: c.label,
      name: field,
      id: c.picker_id|default(''),
      multiple: c.multiple|default(false),
      icon: c.icon|default(''),
      aria_label: 'Change ' ~ spoken ~ '. ' ~ (picked is not empty ? 'Currently ' ~ picked|join(', ') ~ '.' : 'Nothing is set.'),
      value_labels: picked,
      searchable: c.searchable|default(false),
      selected_chips: c.selected_chips|default(false),
      selected: c.selected|default([]),
      open: c.open|default(false),
      options: panel,
    }, with_context = false) }}
  {%- elseif c.kind == 'masked' -%}
    {#- A VALUE THAT MUST NOT BE PRINTED. Normally no value reaches this template
        at all — masking happens upstream in the formatter that reads the field, so
        there is nothing sensitive in the render context to leak, and the dots
        stand for a value this template never saw.

        THE DOTS ARE `role="img"` WITH AN ACCESSIBLE NAME, carried over from the
        component this absorbed. Six bullet characters read aloud are six bullet
        characters; the name is what tells a screen reader that a value exists and
        is withheld. `select-none` keeps them out of a copied selection, where they
        would look like the value. #}
    {%- if c.revealed and c.value is not empty -%}
      <span class="font-mono text-data text-text-light">{{ c.value }}</span>
    {%- else -%}
      <span class="text-meta text-text-faint tracking-widest leading-none select-none" role="img" aria-label="value hidden">••••••</span>
    {%- endif -%}
  {%- elseif c.kind == 'action' -%}
    {#- THE ONE KIND THAT IS NOT A VALUE, and the reason it exists at all. Seven
        kinds render something a record HOLDS; this one renders something a reader
        can DO to the record. It was added deliberately, on the rule this macro
        states above: a shape the kinds cannot express is answered by a new kind
        and never by a slot. The shape that asked for it was a membership's remove
        control — a whole sentence serving as an icon-only button's accessible
        name, which a `text` cell would have printed as prose on every item.

        ITEM-SCOPED, not members-only. It acts on the record the cell belongs to,
        so any listing can carry a row action; nothing here knows what a
        membership is.

        ITS FIELD LIST IS `button`'S, and that is the whole contract — `label`,
        `icon`, `icon_only`, `variant`. There is no `aria_label`: `button` declares
        none, and moves `label` into `aria-label` when the control is icon-only, so
        a second name field would be a second copy of one name that could disagree
        with the first. The `label` of an icon-only action is therefore expected to
        be a SENTENCE naming the effect, which is what
        `action-pattern.md` asks of a single-step action with a server-side one.

        With `href` the control is a link that works without JavaScript; `hx`
        adds the create-trigger htmx attributes, so a row action can open a
        dialog. Without either it stays a `<button type="button">`.

        `ghost` IS THE DEFAULT WEIGHT. A row action is secondary to the row it sits
        on; a listing where every item carries a primary-weight button emphasises
        nothing, which is the same argument the add surface's single `lead` makes. #}
    {%- set action_attributes = create_attribute() -%}
    {%- for key in ['get', 'target', 'select', 'swap'] -%}
      {%- if c.hx[key] is defined and c.hx[key] is not empty -%}
        {%- set action_attributes = action_attributes.setAttribute('data-hx-' ~ key, c.hx[key]) -%}
      {%- endif -%}
    {%- endfor -%}
    {{ include('bb_theme:button', {
      variant: c.variant|default('ghost'),
      type: 'button',
      href: c.href|default(''),
      attributes: action_attributes,
      icon_only: c.icon_only|default(false),
      label: c.label,
      icon: c.icon ? include('bb_theme:icon', { name: c.icon, size: 'sm' }, with_context = false) : null,
    }, with_context = false) }}
  {%- elseif c.kind == 'input' -%}
    {#- AN EDITABLE VALUE, the eleventh kind, for a timesheet cell: a text input
        under the cell's own `name`, carrying the typed text back when the form
        is refused so nothing the reader typed is lost. `label` is its accessible
        name, because a grid cell has no visible label of its own. `error` is
        tied to the input with `aria-describedby` and marks it invalid; `hint`
        names a state the value is in (Proposed); `url` + `url_label` open the
        whole entry; `hidden` carries the entry's identity and version so the
        save can refuse a stale cell. The entry link is an icon in the cell's
        corner, so the input keeps its width; `hx` lets it open the dialog the
        way the action kind does. `stored` and `sums` feed `live_sums`. #}
    {%- set input_id = c.name|replace({'[': '-', ']': ''}) -%}
    {%- set input_attributes = create_attribute()
      .addClass(['text-right', 'tabular-nums'])
      .setAttribute('aria-label', c.label|default(''))
      .setAttribute('inputmode', 'text')
      .setAttribute('size', '5') -%}
    {%- if c.sums is defined -%}
      {%- set input_attributes = input_attributes
        .setAttribute('data-sheet-stored', c.stored|default(0))
        .setAttribute('data-sheet-sums', c.sums|join(' ')) -%}
    {%- endif -%}
    {%- if c.error -%}
      {%- set input_attributes = input_attributes
        .addClass(['border-state-attention-mark'])
        .setAttribute('aria-invalid', 'true')
        .setAttribute('aria-describedby', input_id ~ '--error') -%}
    {%- endif -%}
    {%- if c.autofocus -%}
      {%- set input_attributes = input_attributes.setAttribute('autofocus', 'autofocus') -%}
    {%- endif -%}
    <span class="relative inline-flex flex-col items-end gap-inline-xs w-20 whitespace-normal text-left">
      {{- include('bb_theme:form_input', {
        type: 'text',
        id: input_id,
        name: c.name,
        value: c.value|default(''),
        autocomplete: 'off',
        attributes: input_attributes,
      }, with_context = false) -}}
      {%- for hidden_name, hidden_value in c.hidden|default({}) %}
      <input type="hidden" name="{{ hidden_name }}" value="{{ hidden_value }}" />
      {%- endfor -%}
      {%- if c.hint -%}
      <span class="text-meta text-text-light">{{ c.hint }}</span>
      {%- endif -%}
      {%- if c.url -%}
      {%- set details_attributes = create_attribute()
        .addClass(['absolute', '-top-2', '-right-2', 'grid', 'place-items-center', 'w-4', 'h-4', 'rounded-xs', 'bg-surface', 'text-text-faint', 'hover:text-primary', 'focus-visible:outline-2', 'focus-visible:outline-offset-2', 'focus-visible:outline-focus-ring'])
        .setAttribute('href', c.url)
        .setAttribute('data-hx-boost', 'false')
        .setAttribute('aria-label', c.url_label|default('Details'))
        .setAttribute('title', c.url_label|default('Details')) -%}
      {%- for key in ['get', 'target', 'select', 'swap'] -%}
        {%- if c.hx[key] is defined and c.hx[key] is not empty -%}
          {%- set details_attributes = details_attributes.setAttribute('data-hx-' ~ key, c.hx[key]) -%}
        {%- endif -%}
      {%- endfor -%}
      <a{{ details_attributes }}>{{ include('bb_theme:icon', { name: 'more-horizontal', size: 'xs' }, with_context = false) }}</a>
      {%- endif -%}
      {%- if c.error -%}
      <span id="{{ input_id }}--error" class="text-meta text-state-attention-ink" role="alert">{{ c.error }}</span>
      {%- endif -%}
    </span>
  {%- elseif c.kind == 'time' -%}
    {#- A point in time, already formatted by BbDate. #}
    {{ include('bb_theme:time', { label: c.label, datetime: c.datetime, title: c.title|default('') }, with_context = false) }}
  {%- elseif c.kind == 'rendered' -%}
    {#- MARKUP ANOTHER LAYER ALREADY PRODUCED, and a ninth KIND rather than a
        second slot. The ticket list is bound by core's own `table` views style
        over nine real Views FIELDS, so the face of such a cell is decided by
        that field's FORMATTER and arrives here finished. This template's job
        for it is the same as for the other eight: put it in the `<td>` its
        column asked for, and print it once.

        IT IS PRINTED AND NOT `|raw`-ed. The value is a `MarkupInterface`
        object, which Twig prints unescaped and exactly once; a plain string in
        the same field IS escaped, and that measured difference is what makes
        the object mandatory rather than convenient. `|raw` would unescape
        whatever arrived, including a string this component never inspected, so
        the safety lives in the TYPE of the value and not in a filter here.

        NO GUARD, deliberately. An absent `markup` prints nothing, which is the
        empty cell a column with no value should render — and it is how the
        `Team` column reads honestly while it waits for BB-356 to decide
        whether a ticket has a team at all. The six guarded fields above are
        guarded because they are FORWARDED to a composed component that types
        them as strings; this one is printed, and printing nothing is already
        the right answer. #}
    {{ c.markup }}
  {%- elseif c.exact -%}
    {#- An abbreviated value with its exact value on hover and focus. The
        tooltip opens upwards and right-aligned: the table scrolls sideways, so
        a tooltip below the last row would be clipped, and numbers sit right. -#}
    {%- set tip_id = 'table-exact-' ~ random(1000000) -%}
    <span class="relative inline-flex group" x-data="{ dismissed: false }" @keydown.escape="dismissed = true" @mouseleave="dismissed = false" @focusout="dismissed = false">
      <span tabindex="0" aria-describedby="{{ tip_id }}" class="{{ emphasis_map[c.emphasis]|default('text-text') }} rounded-xs focus-visible:outline-2 focus-visible:outline-offset-2 focus-visible:outline-focus-ring">{{ c.value }}</span>
      <span id="{{ tip_id }}" role="tooltip" class="absolute right-0 bottom-full z-10 mb-inline-xs w-max max-w-prose whitespace-normal text-left rounded-xs bg-text px-inline py-inline-xs text-meta font-normal text-surface opacity-0 pointer-events-none group-hover:opacity-100 group-focus-within:opacity-100" :class="{ '!opacity-0': dismissed }">{{ c.exact }}</span>
    </span>
  {%- else -%}
    {#- `sum` names a live total this value shows, `seconds` its stored base;
        a `live_sums` table rewrites it while the reader types. -#}
    <span class="block max-w-full overflow-hidden text-ellipsis whitespace-pre-line {{ emphasis_map[c.emphasis]|default('text-text') }}"
      {%- if c.sum %} data-sheet-sum="{{ c.sum }}" data-sheet-seconds="{{ c.seconds|default(0) }}"{% endif %}>{{ c.value }}</span>
  {%- endif -%}
{% endmacro %}

{% if variant == 'collapsed' %}
  <div{{ (attributes ?: create_attribute()).addClass(['flex', 'items-center', 'gap-inline', 'p-stack', 'bg-surface-2', 'border', 'border-border-2', 'rounded-sm', 'text-text-light', 'text-data']) }}>
    <span class="w-6 h-6 rounded-full bg-state-running-tint text-state-running-ink grid place-items-center shrink-0"><svg class="w-4 h-4" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="3" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M5 13l4 4L19 7"/></svg></span>
    {% if empty %}{{ empty }}{% else %}Nothing is waiting for a human — every run is running.{% endif %}
  </div>
{% else %}
  {#- THE ROW ACCENT, and the two things it does. The leading rail is drawn on the
      FIRST cell of the row with a pseudo-element, so it costs no `<td>` no column
      asked for. `wait` additionally tints the whole row: a listing nobody is
      acting on is the one state the deleted row components made loud, and the tint
      is what a `wait` badge's `-ink` colour is legible against — the raw hue
      measures 2.76:1 there against the 4.5:1 the contrast criterion asks for. #}
  {% set accent_map = {
    'running': 'bg-state-running',
    'attention': 'bg-state-attention-mark',
    'wait': 'bg-state-unassigned',
    'idle': 'bg-transparent'
  } %}
  {% set rail_classes = ['absolute', 'left-0', 'top-2', 'bottom-2', 'w-1', 'rounded-xs'] %}
  {#- Row height: the default padded row, or a fixed dense (`md`) or roomy (`lg`)
      row whose content is centred without vertical padding, so the row is
      exactly that height. #}
  {% set row_height_map = { 'default': 'h-row py-stack', 'md': 'h-row-md py-0', 'lg': 'h-row-lg py-0' } %}
  {% set row_height_class = row_height_map[row_height|default('default')]|default('h-row py-stack') %}
  {#- LIVE SUMS: a timesheet's totals follow what the reader types, before the
      save, and after a refused save already on render. Each input names its
      totals (`sums`) and the seconds it held; each total shows its base plus
      every change made into it. Unparsable text
      counts nothing, exactly as the server would refuse it. #}
  {% set table_attributes = (attributes ?: create_attribute()).addClass(['w-full', 'border-collapse', 'text-body']) %}
  {% if live_sums %}
    {% set table_attributes = table_attributes
      .setAttribute('x-data', "{ secs(v) { const m = /^(\\d{1,6}):([0-5]\\d)$/.exec((v || '').trim()); return m ? m[1] * 3600 + m[2] * 60 : 0 }, recalc() { const t = {}; this.$root.querySelectorAll('[data-sheet-sums]').forEach((i) => { const d = this.secs(i.value) - Number(i.dataset.sheetStored || 0); i.dataset.sheetSums.split(' ').forEach((k) => { t[k] = (t[k] || 0) + d }) }); this.$root.querySelectorAll('[data-sheet-sum]').forEach((c) => { const s = Math.max(0, Number(c.dataset.sheetSeconds || 0) + (t[c.dataset.sheetSum] || 0)); c.textContent = Math.floor(s / 3600) + ':' + String(Math.floor(s % 3600 / 60)).padStart(2, '0') }) } }")
      .setAttribute('x-init', 'recalc()')
      .setAttribute('x-on:input', 'recalc()') %}
  {% endif %}
  <div class="overflow-x-auto max-w-full">
  <table{{ table_attributes }}>
    <thead>
      {#- A SORTABLE HEADER CELL, shaped like Drupal's own `views-view-table`: the cell
          carries `aria-sort` and the label IS the link that re-sorts, with the
          indicator after it. That is the affordance a Drupal view's table row has —
          a view declares `click_sort_column` on its fields and renders through this
          component — and it is what separates a table from a list here: a list is
          not a table, and a table without a sort is a list with lines.

          `sort` is ABSENT on a column that cannot be sorted, rather than `none`.
          `none` means "sortable, not currently the sort column", so an absent value
          renders plain text and no link — a header link that sorts nothing is worse
          than no affordance, by the same rule the add action follows.

          The indicator is `chevron-down` rotated for ascending rather than a second
          symbol: the sprite ships no `chevron-up`, and `collapsible` already rotates
          a chevron through a class for exactly this reason. It is `aria-hidden`
          through the icon component, because `aria-sort` on the cell already says
          what it shows. #}
      {% set sort_aria = { 'asc': 'ascending', 'desc': 'descending', 'none': 'none' } %}
      <tr>
        {% for column in columns %}
          <th class="text-left text-label text-text-light bg-app px-stack py-stack border-b border-border-2 whitespace-nowrap {{ column.num ? 'text-right tabular-nums' : '' }}"
            {%- if column.sort %} aria-sort="{{ sort_aria[column.sort]|default('none') }}"{% endif %}>
            {%- if column.sort %}
              <a data-table-sort class="inline-flex items-center gap-inline-xs text-inherit no-underline transition-colors duration-fast hover:text-text focus-visible:text-text focus-visible:outline-2 focus-visible:outline-offset-2 focus-visible:outline-focus-ring" href="{{ column.url|default('#') }}">
                {{- column.label -}}
                {%- if column.sort != 'none' %}
                  <span class="inline-flex {{ column.sort == 'asc' ? 'rotate-180' : '' }}">{{ include('bb_theme:icon', { name: 'chevron-down', size: 'xs' }, with_context = false) }}</span>
                {%- endif -%}
              </a>
            {%- else %}{{ column.label }}{% endif -%}
            {#- The space keeps label and sublabel apart in the header's text. #}
            {%- if column.sublabel %} <span class="block text-body-strong text-text">{{ column.sublabel }}</span>{% endif -%}
          </th>
        {% endfor %}
      </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
      {#- ITERATE THE COLUMNS, NOT THE CELLS. The order is the TABLE'S and a row
          does not get to choose one, so a cell for a column that does not exist is
          simply never reached and a column with no cell renders an empty `<td>`.
          That is the difference between a value in the wrong column and a value
          that is absent, and it is the whole reason the cells are keyed. #}
      {% for row in rows %}
        {% set row_classes = ['group', 'transition-colors', 'duration-fast', 'hover:bg-app'] %}
        {% if row.accent == 'wait' %}{% set row_classes = row_classes|merge(['bg-state-unassigned-tint']) %}{% endif %}
        <tr data-table-row class="{{ row_classes|join(' ') }}"{% if row.url %} data-row-url="{{ row.url }}"{% endif %}>
          {% for column in columns %}
            {%- set c = row.cells[column.key]|default(null) -%}
            {#- A row-header column names its row for assistive technology and
                stays at the left edge while the table scrolls sideways. #}
            {%- set cell_tag = column.row_header ? 'th' : 'td' -%}
            <{{ cell_tag }}{% if column.row_header %} scope="row"{% endif %} class="{{ row_height_class }} px-stack align-middle whitespace-nowrap border-b border-border-2 {{ loop.first ? 'relative pl-stack' : '' }} {{ column.num ? 'text-right tabular-nums' : '' }} {{ column.row_header ? 'sticky left-0 z-10 [&:has([data-option-picker-trigger][aria-expanded=true])]:z-20 bg-surface text-left font-normal' : '' }}">
              {%- if loop.first %}<span class="{{ (rail_classes|merge([accent_map[row.accent]|default('bg-transparent')]))|join(' ') }}" aria-hidden="true"></span>{% endif -%}
              {%- if column.row_header and row.depth is defined -%}
                {#- A TREE ROW: indented one step per level, its disclosure a plain
                    link that toggles the row (no script), and a slot of the same
                    width on a row without children so names stay aligned. #}
                {%- set indent_map = { 1: 'pl-gutter', 2: 'pl-12', 3: 'pl-18' } -%}
                <span class="inline-flex items-center gap-inline {{ indent_map[row.depth]|default('') }}">
                  {%- if row.disclosure -%}
                    <a data-table-disclosure class="inline-flex w-5 justify-center text-text-light no-underline transition-colors duration-fast hover:text-text focus-visible:text-text focus-visible:outline-2 focus-visible:outline-offset-2 focus-visible:outline-focus-ring" href="{{ row.disclosure.href }}" aria-expanded="{{ row.disclosure.expanded ? 'true' : 'false' }}" aria-label="{{ row.disclosure.expanded ? 'Collapse' : 'Expand' }} {{ c.value|default('') }}">
                      <span class="inline-flex {{ row.disclosure.expanded ? '' : '-rotate-90' }}">{{ include('bb_theme:icon', { name: 'chevron-down', size: 'xs' }, with_context = false) }}</span>
                    </a>
                  {%- else -%}
                    <span class="inline-flex w-5" aria-hidden="true"></span>
                  {%- endif -%}
                  {%- if c %}{{ cells.cell(c) }}{% endif -%}
                  {%- for badge in row.badges|default([]) -%}
                    {{ include('bb_theme:badge', { label: badge.label, tone: badge.tone|default('neutral') }, with_context = false) }}
                  {%- endfor -%}
                </span>
              {%- elseif c %}{{ cells.cell(c) }}{% endif -%}
            </{{ cell_tag }}>
          {% endfor %}
        </tr>
      {% endfor %}
    </tbody>
  </table>
  </div>
{% endif %}
